 five pence ( eight U.S. cents ) to a near-record 720 pence ( $ 11.60 ) in late <ei32 trading> on London 's stock exchange yesterday . Since Tuesday , the shares have <ei35 gained> nearly 4 % . But an all-out bidding <ei36 war> between the world 's top auto giants for Britain 's leading luxury-car maker seems unlikely . \" We will not <ei37 go> over a certain level , \" <ei38 said> David N. McCammon , Ford 's vice president for finance , at a news <ei39 conference> yesterday in Dearborn , Mich . \" There 's some price at which we 'd <ei41 stop> <ei42 bidding> . \" He would n't <ei43 specify> what it was . And powerful political <ei44 pressures> may <ei45 convince> the Conservative government to <ei46 keep> its so-called golden share , which limits any individual holding to 15 % , until the restriction <ei47 expires> on Dec . 31 , 1990 . \" I really do n't <ei49 see> the government <ei50 doing> something that Jaguar does n't <ei51 want> over the next 14 months , \" <ei53 said> Kenneth Warren , a Conservative member of Parliament and chairman of the Select Committee on Trade and Industry in Britain 's House of Commons . \" The golden share is a single share , but it is the magic share . \" The government <ei54 retained> the single share after <ei55 selling> its stake in Jaguar in 1984 -- part of a nationalistic practice of protecting former government-owned enterprises to deflect criticism of privatization . The 15 % restriction covers any would-be suitor , British or foreign . Ford is <ei57 willing> to <ei58 bid> for 100 % of Jaguar 's shares if both the government and Jaguar shareholders <ei59 agree> to <ei60 relax> the anti-takeover barrier prematurely . As Jaguar 's biggest holder and Britain 's biggest car maker , Ford could <ei61 turn> up the heat by <ei62 convening> a special shareholders ' <ei63 meeting> and <ei64 urging> holders to <ei65 drop> the limits early . Ford might <ei66 succeed> because many shareholders <ei68 are> speculators <ei69 keen> for a full <ei70 bid> or institutional investors <ei71 unhappy> over Jaguar management 's <ei72 handling> of its current financial <ei74 difficulties> . The government probably would n't <ei75 give> in readily to a hostile foray by Ford , however . It has <ei76 relinquished> a golden share only once before -- during British Petroleum Co.'s # 2.5 billion ( $ 4 billion ) <ei77 takeover> of Britoil PLC in 1988 . In <ei79 wooing> British lawmakers , GM has <ei80 pointed> out that its <ei81 willingness> to <ei82 settle> for a minority stake would <ei83 keep> Jaguar <ei84 British-owned> and <ei85 independent> . This week , the U.S. auto giant <ei87 paid> for 10 House of Commons members and two House of Lords members to <ei88 fly> to Detroit and <ei89 tour> its operations there . While the <ei90 visit> was unrelated to Jaguar , GM Chairman Roger Smith <ei91 answered> the legislators ' <ei92 questions> about it over <ei93 lunch> Tuesday . He <ei95 said> Jaguar \" should n't be <ei96 smothered> by anyone else , \" <ei97 recalled> one participant . Politics also influences the government 's thinking on the anti-takeover restriction . The Conservatives do n't dare jeopardize marginal Tory seats in Coventry , where Jaguar has headquarters , nor can the government easily back down on promised protection for a privatized company while it proceeds with controversial plans to privatize most of Britain 's water and electricity industries . Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher might , however , be <ei98 receptive> to any <ei99 request> by Jaguar Chairman Sir John Egan for the restriction 's early <ei100 removal> to let GM <ei101 amass> more than 15 % or <ei102 mount> a friendly suitor <ei103 bid> against Ford . In the end , Sir John -- rather than the government or Jaguar shareholders -- may hold the key that unlocks the golden share .", "gold": [["ei23", "causes", "ei21"], ["ei44", "causes", "ei46"], ["ei68", "causes", "ei66"], ["ei81", "causes", "ei84"], ["ei81", "causes", "ei85"], ["ei100", "cau